  "guideNakedSingle": "A cell is a naked single when every digit but one is already ruled out by its row, column and box. The one digit left has to go there.",
  "guideHiddenSingle": "A digit is a hidden single in a unit (row, column or box) when only one cell in that unit can still take it. Even if that cell has other candidates, the digit has to go there.",
  "guideNakedPair": "When two cells in a unit can only hold the same two digits, those digits have to go in those two cells, in one order or the other. So they can be removed from every other cell in that unit.",
  "guidePointing": "When all the remaining spots for a digit in one unit lie on a line shared with another unit (a box and a row, say), the digit has to go on that line. So it can be removed from the rest of the other unit.",
  "guideHiddenPair": "When two digits can only go in the same two cells of a unit, those cells have to hold exactly those digits. Every other candidate in those two cells can be removed.",
  "guideNakedTriple": "When three cells in a unit hold only three digits between them, those digits belong to those cells. They can be removed from every other cell in that unit.",
  "guideXWing": "When a digit can only go in the same two columns in two different rows, it ends up in two opposite corners of a rectangle. So it can be removed from the rest of those two columns (and the same works with rows and columns swapped).",
  "guideXYWing": "A cell with candidates A and B sees two cells holding A+C and B+C. Whichever way the first cell turns out, C lands in one of the other two. So C can be removed from any cell that sees both of them.",
  "guideSwordfish": "Like an X-Wing, but across three rows and three columns: if a digit in three rows is confined to the same three columns, it can be removed from the rest of those columns (and likewise with rows and columns swapped).",
